      "narrative": "Maricopa County’s residential market is currently flat to slightly soft on prices: recent county-level analyses and ARMLS-based reports show overall median sale prices around the mid-$400,000s, with July medians either unchanged month-over-month or down marginally year-over-year.[2][4][12] Inventory has been edging down, with active listings and months of supply slipping slightly compared with last year, even as closed sales ticked up modestly, signaling a gradual move away from the more oversupplied conditions of 2025.[2][3][4][5] Buyer-seller dynamics are balanced but cooling on the demand side: most homes sell below original list price, days on market have risen into the roughly two-to-three-month range, and pending sales and new listings have both declined, pointing to less competition and fewer bidding wars outside the most affordable price bands.[3][5][7][12] Locally, the market is being shaped by continued in-migration and job growth around Phoenix’s major employers and construction corridors, but also by higher mortgage rates and a pullback in new-home activity, with resale transactions now driving most of the county’s closings.[6][12][13]",
